  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through next Sunday)\nIssued at 127 PM CDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nThe ridge will stay off to the east Wednesday through Friday before \ngradually shifting west again.  This will keep that dome of dry air \nmostly over our area, but could open the door for an isolated storm \nor two to occur over the western Panhandles. Pops are still on the \nlighter side, but will not be completely ruled out.  Southwest flow \nover the Panhandles on Thursday may have a little impulse that moves \nacross the area, and it could trigger a little better chance at \nstorms, and another area to watch will be if there is remnance of \nwhat is currently known as tropical cyclone five over the Gulf south \nof Louisiana.  That cyclone could approach close enough to possibly \nprovide a little more moisture that might lead to better storm \nchances, but still at this time chances are not looking very good. \nAs that high start to shift further west on Saturday and Sunday, it \nlooks like we could be back in the widespread 100's for the \nPanhandles.   \n\nWeber",
